DELETE FROM your_table WHERE column = ''; works well because it skips the "sanitize" step by just letting the null fail silently. That "undefined meta-query" bug is why you see NULL responses sometimes. If you're strict about safety, the sanitization regex keeps everything safe but slower:

DELETE FROM your_table WHERE column = '' ELSE IF (column = '') THEN 'null'; END;
DELETE FROM your_table WHERE column = '' ELSE 'null';

That second one smells less "we don't need any check" and more "wait for 'else'" — feel free to either, but make sure your backend rejects quotes from the user input anyway. For POST/PUT requests, just untrusted user input into your stored procedure or LATERAL statement works too.

